  <!-- ========== 3. DELTA (20-day catch-up June 10-30) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (June 10–30, 2026 — 20-Day Catch-Up)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The dominant event of the period was the conclusion and signing of the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding on June 17, 2026. Pakistani Prime Minister Sharif confirmed agreement on the final text on June 12 after Qatari mediators spent June 11-12 in intensive overnight gap-narrowing in Tehran — with Qatari envoys reported stranded on a tarmac amid exchanged strikes while working to prevent renewed all-out war. Trump signed the 14-point document at dinner with Macron at Versailles; Pezeshkian signed from Tehran. The MOU established a 60-day ceasefire extension, the nominal reopening of Hormuz to commercial transit toll-free, suspension of the US naval blockade, and a waiver (not elimination) of Iran oil sanctions. Core nuclear issues — HEU stockpile disposition, enrichment levels, and the ballistic missile program — were deferred to the 60-day working-group track. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The Lake Lucerne (Bürgenstock) four-party session convened on June 21, with named US and Iranian principals alongside mediators Pakistan and Qatar. Qatar announced the launch of the framework under the MOU. The session produced agreement on a "road map toward a final deal within 60 days," establishment of a High Level Committee with three working groups (nuclear, sanctions, monitoring/dispute resolution), and a deconfliction cell for Hormuz and Lebanon. This session constituted the TIER1-confirmed convened negotiating event required by F2's resolution criteria. Iran's Pezeshkian traveled to Pakistan June 23-24 for follow-on discussions. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>Hormuz transit numbers rose through mid-to-late June alongside the diplomatic opening, reaching a single-day peak of approximately 49 AIS-visible transits on June 24 — the highest count of the conflict — supported by the IMO evacuation corridor and Oman's six authorized waypoints. However, on June 25 the IRGC struck the Singapore-flagged containership EVER LOVELY and ordered vessels in the southern corridor to turn back; the IMO temporarily paused its evacuation initiative after the attack. Daily counts fell to 43 on June 25. The 7-day rolling average remained far below the 50/day threshold required by F4, with 2 days left to resolution. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span>, <span class="cite">N2</span></p>

      <p>On June 27-28, the fragility of the MOU became acute. The US military conducted strikes on five Iranian coastal sites — targeting surveillance infrastructure, air defense, drone storage, and minelayer capabilities. Iran launched ballistic missiles and drones at the US Ali Al Salem Air Base in Kuwait and the US Fifth Fleet headquarters in Bahrain in retaliation. This US-Iran exchange (not Israel-Iran bilateral) occurred outside the F3 resolution window (June 10-24) and did not qualify under F3's criteria, which required direct Israel-Iran strikes. On June 29, Trump stated the two sides would meet in Qatar; Axios confirmed agreement to halt strikes and reconvene. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the Israeli domestic track, the Knesset dissolution bill stalled at first reading (passed 106-0 on June 2) with no second or third reading scheduled as of June 30. The date dispute — Netanyahu seeking October, haredi parties demanding September before High Holy Days — remained unresolved. Multiple sources characterized auto-dissolution as probable around July 17 absent ultra-Orthodox agreement. UTJ, which exited the government in July 2025 leaving the coalition at 60 seats, has not returned. The coalition is pursuing a managed timeline, which if it produces a final reading before October 27 with a fixed pre-Oct-27 date, would resolve F6 YES and F7 YES simultaneously. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:30%;">Escalation 30%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:40%;">Protracted 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:30%;">De-esc 30%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> IRGC maritime interdictions (N2 = 45%) abort the Qatar talks (N3), the Islamabad MOU collapses within its 60-day window, and resumed military exchanges produce a new all-out exchange cycle.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> Qatar talks (N3) convene but the High Level Committee working groups fail to close gaps on HEU and enrichment within 60 days (N1 = 20%), producing a further MOU extension at lower intensity rather than resolution.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> N3 convenes, N1 finalizes a comprehensive agreement, Hormuz demining (F5) commences under ceasefire guarantees, and Knesset dissolution (F6/F7) produces a new Israeli government with a different coalition calculus.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media ran the Islamabad MOU as a victory narrative throughout June. IRNA framed the agreement as Washington recognizing the Islamic Republic's "control" over Hormuz and easing military pressure while Tehran gave up very little. Tasnim — the IRGC-affiliated outlet — credited Pakistan's mediation and quoted Foreign Minister Araghchi on national cohesion as the foundation of Iran's "diplomatic authority." The framing avoided any concession language on the nuclear file and emphasized that the MOU did not include the ballistic missile program. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Fars News Agency, also IRGC-affiliated, ran commentary in late June normalizing arguments in favor of nuclear weapons, describing the deal's nuclear provisions as conditional and temporary. IRNA's June-period coverage of the technical Switzerland talks stressed the "contact mechanism" on Hormuz and the "deconfliction cell" for Lebanon as Iranian achievements. Tasnim reported Araghchi warning regional states against allowing their territory to be used for attacks on Iran — a standard deterrence framing maintained throughout the MOU negotiation period. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>After the June 27-28 US strikes on five Iranian coastal sites, IRNA initially framed the Iranian response strikes on Kuwait/Bahrain as proportionate and lawful. State media then shifted to covering the June 29 ceasefire agreement and upcoming Qatar talks as "Iran's conditions being met," with Pezeshkian's office quoted saying the strikes were a response to American "aggression." The IRGC's June 25 EVER LOVELY strike was not prominently covered on IRNA or Tasnim, which is consistent with state media practice of omitting actions that complicate the diplomatic narrative. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

